<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8' standalone='no'?>
<module referenceFile="part.arduino_mkr_wifi_1010.fzp" moduleId="com.greenshoegarage.arduino.mkr-wifi-1010-tht-v1" fritzingVersion="1.0.2">
  <version>1.0.0</version>
  <author>Built for Green Shoe Garage</author>
  <title>Arduino MKR WiFi 1010</title>
  <label>MKR</label>
  <date>2026-07-27</date>
  <url>https://store.arduino.cc/products/arduino-mkr-wifi-1010</url>
  <description>Arduino MKR WiFi 1010 (SKU ABX00023), a MKR form factor board built around a Microchip SAMD21G18A Arm Cortex-M0+ at 48 MHz, a u-blox NINA-W102 radio (WiFi, Bluetooth and BLE) and a Microchip ATECC508A secure element. Two facts bite people who wire this board up from memory. First, the I/O is 3.3 V and is NOT 5 V tolerant, so driving any pin from a 5 V signal will damage the board. Second, the +5V pin (header pin 28) is not a regulated output: it is jumper connected to the USB power input, so it only carries voltage when the board is USB powered. Header pin numbering runs 1 to 14 along the analog row starting at the USB end, then 15 to 28 along the digital and power row running from the far end back toward the USB end, so header pin 28 (+5V) sits opposite header pin 1 (AREF). Part version 1.0.0.</description>
  <tags>
    <tag>arduino</tag>
    <tag>MKR</tag>
    <tag>SAMD21</tag>
    <tag>WiFi</tag>
    <tag>Bluetooth</tag>
    <tag>BLE</tag>
    <tag>IoT</tag>
    <tag>NINA-W102</tag>
    <tag>ATECC508A</tag>
    <tag>ABX00023</tag>
  </tags>
